    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ── Moran's I ── -->
  <section class="tool-section" id="morans">
    <div class="section-label">Section 02</div>
    <h2 class="section-title">Moran's I in plain English</h2>

    <div class="tool-cols">
      <div class="prose">
        <p>Moran's I is the one statistic to learn first. It behaves like a correlation coefficient — but instead of correlating two variables, it correlates a variable with itself across space.</p>
        <p>Range: roughly −1 to +1. Expected value under no spatial pattern is a tiny negative number, −1/(n−1), close to zero for any decent sample size.</p>
        <p>Read it like this:</p>
        <div class="callout"><strong>+0.6 to +1.0</strong> — strong clustering. Hot spots are real.</div>
        <div class="callout"><strong>+0.1 to +0.5</strong> — moderate clustering. Worth a closer look.</div>
        <div class="callout"><strong>Near 0</strong> — random. Don't read shapes into noise.</div>
        <div class="callout"><strong>Below 0</strong> — dispersion. Ask why.</div>
      </div>

      <div class="tool-panel">
        <div class="tool-panel__label">The formula</div>
        <div class="tool-code">      n     Σᵢ Σⱼ wᵢⱼ (xᵢ − x̄)(xⱼ − x̄)
I = ───  · ─────────────────────────
      W           Σᵢ (xᵢ − x̄)²</div>
        <h3 class="subhead">What each piece does</h3>
        <div class="prose">
          <p><strong>wᵢⱼ</strong> — the spatial weights matrix. Says which locations count as neighbors of which.</p>
          <p><strong>(xᵢ − x̄)(xⱼ − x̄)</strong> — the cross-product. Positive when both locations are on the same side of the mean.</p>
          <p><strong>n / W</strong> — a normalization so I sits roughly between −1 and +1.</p>
          <p><strong>In one line:</strong> sum up how often a place and its neighbors deviate from the mean in the same direction. Divide by total variance. That's it.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ── LISA ── -->
  <section class="tool-section" id="lisa">
    <div class="section-label">Section 03</div>
    <h2 class="section-title">LISA — local indicators of spatial association</h2>
    <div class="section-description">Moran's I gives you one number for the whole map. LISA gives you a label for every single location. That's the difference between knowing a city has crime hot spots and knowing exactly which blocks are which.</div>

    <div class="tool-cols">
      <div>
        <div class="tool-plate"><canvas id="lisaMap" width="320" height="320" aria-label="LISA cluster map: each cell classified as high-high, low-low, high-low, low-high, or not significant"></canvas></div>
        <div class="tool-legend">
          <div class="tool-legend__item"><span class="tool-legend__swatch" style="background: var(--lisa-hh)"></span>High-High</div>
          <div class="tool-legend__item"><span class="tool-legend__swatch" style="background: var(--lisa-ll)"></span>Low-Low</div>
          <div class="tool-legend__item"><span class="tool-legend__swatch" style="background: var(--lisa-hl)"></span>High-Low</div>
          <div class="tool-legend__item"><span class="tool-legend__swatch" style="background: var(--lisa-lh)"></span>Low-High</div>
          <div class="tool-legend__item"><span class="tool-legend__swatch" style="background: var(--lisa-ns)"></span>Not significant</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h3 class="subhead">Four categories, one outlier class</h3>
        <div class="prose">
          <p><strong>High-High (HH)</strong> — a high value surrounded by other high values. The actual hot spot.</p>
          <p><strong>Low-Low (LL)</strong> — a low value surrounded by low values. The cold spot.</p>
          <p><strong>High-Low (HL)</strong> — a high value among low neighbors. A spatial outlier worth investigating.</p>
          <p><strong>Low-High (LH)</strong> — the opposite outlier. A pocket of low values inside an otherwise high region.</p>
          <p>For audience segmentation, LISA is the move. It surfaces look-alike clusters and the unusual cells that don't fit the local pattern — exactly the two things a segmentation product needs to flag.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

