  <!-- ── Color ── -->
  <section class="tool-section" id="color">
    <div class="section-label">Section 05</div>
    <h2 class="section-title">ColorBrewer and CIEDE2000</h2>
    <div class="section-description">Two questions you raised, two distinct answers. ColorBrewer is directly in the workflow. CIEDE2000 sits one layer above it.</div>

    <div class="tool-cols">
      <div>
        <h3 class="subhead">ColorBrewer — directly related</h3>
        <div class="prose">
          <p>Cynthia Brewer's palette library is the de facto standard for choropleth maps. Spatial autocorrelation visuals lean on it constantly. Three scheme types, three jobs:</p>
        </div>

        <div class="tool-swatch-group">
          <div class="tool-swatches" id="palSeq"></div>
          <div class="tool-swatch-name">Sequential — YlGnBu — for raw values</div>
        </div>
        <div class="tool-swatch-group">
          <div class="tool-swatches" id="palDiv"></div>
          <div class="tool-swatch-name">Diverging — RdBu — for deviations from a midpoint</div>
        </div>
        <div class="tool-swatch-group">
          <div class="tool-swatches" id="palQual"></div>
          <div class="tool-swatch-name">Qualitative — Set1 — for LISA categories</div>
        </div>

        <p class="note-line">All three are baked into <code>tmap</code>, <code>geopandas</code>, <code>ggplot2</code>, ArcGIS, and QGIS. Use them. Don't invent palettes for spatial work.</p>
      </div>

      <div>
        <h3 class="subhead">CIEDE2000 — one layer above</h3>
        <div class="prose">
          <p>CIEDE2000 is the CIE's perceptual color-difference formula, ΔE2000. It tells you how distinguishable two colors are to the human eye.</p>
          <p>It doesn't appear in spatial statistics literature. It appears in <strong>cartographic design</strong>, especially when someone is building a custom bivariate palette and needs to prove that adjacent cells in the legend are perceptually distinct enough to read separately.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="tool-panel__label" style="margin-top: var(--space-3)">Distinguishable vs. not</div>
        <div class="sa-delta">
          <div class="sa-delta__pair"><div style="background: #2c5f8f"></div><div style="background: #2f6294"></div></div>
          <div>
            <div class="sa-delta__num">ΔE ≈ 2</div>
            <div class="sa-delta__label">Imperceptible to most viewers</div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="sa-delta">
          <div class="sa-delta__pair"><div style="background: #2c5f8f"></div><div style="background: #6c9bc4"></div></div>
          <div>
            <div class="sa-delta__num">ΔE ≈ 18</div>
            <div class="sa-delta__label">Easily distinguished</div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="sa-delta">
          <div class="sa-delta__pair"><div style="background: #2c5f8f"></div><div style="background: #f06b1d"></div></div>
          <div>
            <div class="sa-delta__num">ΔE ≈ 60</div>
            <div class="sa-delta__label">Clearly different categories</div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <p class="note-line">Rule of thumb: ΔE under 2 is invisible, 2–10 noticeable, above 10 clearly different. ColorBrewer schemes are engineered to keep adjacent colors above the threshold.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ── Starter project ── -->
  <section class="tool-section" id="project">
    <div class="section-label">Section 06</div>
    <h2 class="section-title">Learn by doing — an afternoon project</h2>
    <div class="section-description">Median household income by US county. The data is clean, the spatial pattern is loud, and you'll recognize the clusters on sight.</div>

    <h3 class="subhead">Pick your path</h3>
    <div class="tool-table-wrap">
      <table class="tool-table">
        <thead><tr><th>Tool</th><th>Best for</th><th>Time to first map</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>GeoDa (free GUI)</td><td>Designers, click-driven learning, fastest insight</td><td>~30 min</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Python + GeoPandas + PySAL</td><td>Reusable notebooks, integration with other workflows</td><td>~2 hr</td></tr>
          <tr><td>R + sf + spdep</td><td>Stats community standard, best documentation</td><td>~2 hr</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <div class="prose">
      <p><strong>Recommended sequence for you:</strong> open GeoDa first — Luc Anselin (who invented LISA) built it as a teaching tool, and it'll get you to a finished cluster map in half an hour. Then port the analysis to Python so you have a reusable notebook.</p>
    </div>

    <h3 class="subhead">The seven steps</h3>
    <ol class="steps" role="list">
      <li class="step">
        <div>
          <h4 class="step__title">Get the geometry</h4>
          <div class="step__body">Download the US counties shapefile from Census TIGER/Line.</div>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li class="step">
        <div>
          <h4 class="step__title">Join the data</h4>
          <div class="step__body">Pull median household income from ACS 5-year estimates, table B19013.</div>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li class="step">
        <div>
          <h4 class="step__title">Choropleth first</h4>
          <div class="step__body">ColorBrewer YlGnBu, 5 classes, quantile breaks. Look before you measure.</div>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li class="step">
        <div>
          <h4 class="step__title">Define neighbors</h4>
          <div class="step__body">Queen contiguity is the safe default for county polygons.</div>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li class="step">
        <div>
          <h4 class="step__title">Run Moran's I</h4>
          <div class="step__body">Permutation test, 999 reps. Expect a value near +0.6.</div>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li class="step">
        <div>
          <h4 class="step__title">Compute LISA</h4>
          <div class="step__body">Produce a cluster map with the standard 5-color legend.</div>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li class="step">
        <div>
          <h4 class="step__title">Write it up</h4>
          <div class="step__body">Three bullets in plain English: where the hot spots are, where the cold spots are, what's surprising.</div>
        </div>
      </li>
    </ol>

    <h3 class="subhead">Python starter — copy and run</h3>
    <div class="tool-code"><button class="tool-copy" type="button">Copy</button>import geopandas as gpd
from libpysal.weights import Queen
from esda.moran import Moran, Moran_Local
import matplotlib.pyplot as plt

# 1. Load counties + income (assumes you've joined them)
gdf = gpd.read_file("us_counties_income.geojson")

# 2. Spatial weights
w = Queen.from_dataframe(gdf)
w.transform = "r"   # row-standardize

# 3. Global Moran's I
mi = Moran(gdf["median_income"], w, permutations=999)
print(f"Moran's I = {mi.I:.3f}, p = {mi.p_sim:.4f}")

# 4. Local Moran's I (LISA)
lisa = Moran_Local(gdf["median_income"], w, permutations=999)

# 5. Plot the cluster map
from splot.esda import lisa_cluster
fig, ax = plt.subplots(figsize=(12, 8))
lisa_cluster(lisa, gdf, p=0.05, ax=ax)
plt.show()</div>

    <h3 class="subhead">Stretch — make it about your job</h3>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>Swap counties for ZIP codes or DMAs. Swap median income for campaign open rate, revenue per recipient, or list growth rate. The workflow is identical. The patterns you surface should directly shape how a segmentation product recommends "look-alike geos" or flags "underperforming neighborhoods."</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ── Prompt library ── -->
  <section class="tool-section" id="prompts">
    <div class="section-label">Section 07</div>
    <h2 class="section-title">Prompt blocks for agentic workflows</h2>
    <div class="section-description">Three reusable prompts. Drop them into a system prompt, a skill description, or a research note. Each one is opinionated about defaults so the agent doesn't ask trivial questions.</div>

    <h3 class="subhead">1. Spatial analyst agent</h3>
    <div class="tool-code"><button class="tool-copy" type="button">Copy</button>You are a spatial statistics analyst. When given geographic data
with values per location, work through these steps in order:

1. Confirm the spatial unit (ZIP, tract, county, hex bin) and the
   variable you're analyzing.
2. Define the spatial weights matrix. Default to queen contiguity
   for polygons, k=8 for points. Ask only if the default is wrong.
3. Compute global Moran's I. Report:
   - the I value
   - its expected value under the null
   - the pseudo p-value from 999 permutations
   - one sentence interpreting the result in plain English
4. Compute LISA. Produce a cluster map labeling each unit as
   HH, LL, HL, LH, or not significant (p > 0.05).
5. Surface the three most actionable clusters. For each, write one
   line of "what to do about it" tied to the user's domain.

Use ColorBrewer's RdBu for the variable's choropleth. Use the
standard LISA qualitative palette (red HH, blue LL, light red LH,
light blue HL, grey ns) for the cluster map. Don't invent custom
palettes unless asked.

Never call a pattern "clustered" or "dispersed" without a p-value
to back it up.</div>

    <h3 class="subhead">2. Translate results for a stakeholder</h3>
    <div class="tool-code"><button class="tool-copy" type="button">Copy</button>You're translating a spatial autocorrelation analysis for a
product or marketing audience. Avoid "Moran," "Geary," and "LISA"
in the body — name them only in a footnote.

Structure your output as:
- Headline: one sentence on whether nearby locations behave alike,
  with a confidence qualifier (strong / moderate / weak / none).
- What it means: two sentences in domain language.
- Where the action is: 2–4 named clusters or outlier locations.
- One recommendation: a single move the team can make on this.

Show one supporting visual: a choropleth of the variable OR a LISA
cluster map. Caption it in 12 words or fewer.</div>

    <h3 class="subhead">3. Teach the concept to a teammate</h3>
    <div class="tool-code"><button class="tool-copy" type="button">Copy</button>You are an instructional designer explaining spatial autocorrelation
to someone with a data background but no spatial-stats training.

Open with Tobler's law as a single sentence. Then walk through
three regimes — positive, none, negative — and show what each
looks like on a 6×6 grid using ColorBrewer RdBu.

Introduce Moran's I as "a correlation coefficient between a
variable and its spatial neighbors." Skip the formula on the first
pass; offer it as a follow-up if asked.

Introduce LISA as "Moran's I per location, with a clustering label."
Use HH/LL/HL/LH and define each in one line.

End with one starter project the learner could complete in an
afternoon. Default to median household income by US county unless
the user names a domain.

Don't use the words "intuitively," "essentially," or "simply."</div>
  </section>

  <!-- ── Glossary ── -->
  <section class="tool-section" id="glossary">
    <div class="section-label">Section 08</div>
    <h2 class="section-title">Compact glossary</h2>

    <dl class="sa-glossary">
      <dt>Spatial autocorrelation</dt>
      <dd>Similarity of values among neighboring locations, measured against a null of randomness.</dd>

      <dt>Tobler's First Law</dt>
      <dd>"Everything is related to everything else, but near things are more related than distant things."</dd>

      <dt>Moran's I</dt>
      <dd>Global scalar measure of spatial autocorrelation. Roughly −1 to +1.</dd>

      <dt>Geary's C</dt>
      <dd>Alternative global measure. Inverted scale: 0 means clustered, 1 means random.</dd>

      <dt>LISA</dt>
      <dd>Local Indicators of Spatial Association. Local Moran's I per location. Yields HH, LL, HL, LH classes.</dd>

      <dt>Getis-Ord Gi*</dt>
      <dd>Local hot spot / cold spot statistic, expressed as a z-score.</dd>

      <dt>Spatial weights matrix (W)</dt>
      <dd>The definition of "neighbor." Determines which locations contribute to each other's local measures.</dd>

      <dt>Queen contiguity</dt>
      <dd>Neighbors share an edge or a corner. The safe default for irregular polygons.</dd>

      <dt>Rook contiguity</dt>
      <dd>Neighbors share an edge only. Stricter than queen.</dd>

      <dt>Choropleth</dt>
      <dd>A map shaded by a value per region.</dd>

      <dt>Bivariate choropleth</dt>
      <dd>A map encoding two variables at once via a color grid. Sometimes called a spatial weave.</dd>

      <dt>ColorBrewer</dt>
      <dd>Cynthia Brewer's palette library. Three scheme types: sequential, diverging, qualitative.</dd>

      <dt>CIEDE2000 (ΔE2000)</dt>
      <dd>The CIE's perceptual color-difference formula. Tells you whether two colors are distinguishable.</dd>

      <dt>Gaussian random field</dt>
      <dd>A statistical model that generates spatially smooth random data. Used to simulate autocorrelated values.</dd>
    </dl>
  </section>

